-- chem/cwbound.hypercubical — the electron's lowest level on the walled cage bracketed rigorously, for free t and L, by
-- the discrete Collatz–Wielandt principle: for H = L·diag(U) − t·A with A the cage's adjacency (t > 0), −H + cI is a
-- nonnegative matrix for large c, so its Perron vector is the lowest state of H and is positive, and for every
-- positive vector ψ,  min_u (Hψ)_u/ψ_u ≤ E₀ ≤ max_u (Hψ)_u/ψ_u. With ψ the constant vector (Hψ)_u/ψ_u = L·U(u) −
-- t·deg(u), and since the minimum of a sum is at least the sum of the minima and likewise for the maximum,
--   L·(min_u U) − t·(max_u deg) ≤ E₀ ≤ L·(max_u U) − t·(min_u deg).
-- Here U(u) = G(u,u) − 2G(a,u) − 2G(b,u) is the electron's potential (chem/h2plus-net.hypercubical), in units of 1/280,
-- and deg is 3 on the cage's four three-line vertices and 2 on the six two-line ones. Checked: for the nuclei four
-- lines apart (4, 9) min 280·U = −86 and max = 54 (`cw-far`); for the nuclei two lines apart (4, 5) min 280·U = −96 and
-- max = 72 (`cw-near`); the degree extremes are 2 and 3 (`cw-deg`). So, with L = λ/2,
--   nuclei 4,9:  −43L/140 − 3t ≤ E₀ ≤ 27L/140 − 2t ;   nuclei 4,5:  −12L/35 − 3t ≤ E₀ ≤ 9L/35 − 2t.
-- The lower end is a rigorous bound on the bond's depth with no dual-torus or eigenvalue hand-step, finite and
-- checked; a non-constant ψ, larger on the nucleus vertices, tightens the hop term from −t·max deg toward −t·deg at
-- the nucleus. The Collatz–Wielandt principle itself (Perron–Frobenius for −H + cI) is stated, standard. A leaf.
import "h2plus-net.hypercubical"

